{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789231731,"nonce":"hJetZVuCpJyqGfPb53KyNkUKqQ4=","title":"Multi-line block bodies return their last expression instead of null","message":"A method, constructor or closure body written as a block (newline after the opening brace) returns null in the reference implementation unless it has an explicit return. WrenLift returns the value of a trailing expression statement, including an assignment or a variable declaration:\n\nclass A { one(x) {\n    _k = x\n  } }\nA.new().one(3)  // wren_cli: null, wlift: 3\n\nSame for Fn.new { |x|\n  x + 1\n}. Single-line bodies ({ x + 1 }) are correct in both. Interpreter and tiered agree, so this is in the parser or MIR builder: block bodies must lower to an implicit return null; only the single-line expression form returns the expression.","files":null}]}